# spade determinism corpus v0 -- scenario: two_world_isolation
#
# Two worlds at OVERLAPPING COORDINATES: world 0 holds a dense eight-body
# cluster around (0, 1, 0), world 1 holds two bodies at coordinates inside
# that cluster. 300 steps of 2 ms at 2 substeps, uniform materials, so the
# broad phase really is one batched sweep across both worlds. If a range
# check or a cell key leaked, world 1's pair would be shoved by neighbours it
# cannot see.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS. The 64-bit FNV-1a digest of every registered state byte at
# the end of the scenario, as defined by engine/testing/replay.hpp's
# state_digest(): the tick, then each registered array's name, element size,
# world count, per-world capacity and payload, folded in the state registry's
# walk order. The scenario itself is built in code by
# tests/test_determinism.cpp (two_world_isolation_scenario()) -- there is no world file involved
# (S5 owns those), so this digest and that function are the whole definition.
#
# WHEN IT CHANGES. Any change to a pinned op order (integrator, contacts, grid
# sweep, Dryden coefficients), to the rng construction, to a state layout
# offset, to the pass schedule's order or contents, to the array registration
# order or names, or to the scenario's own parameters. Every one of those is a
# deliberate, reviewable act; NONE of them is a drive-by. Regenerating this
# file without recording why in this header defeats the point of committing it.
## REGENERATED 2026-08-11 -- the platform-libm parity fix (PR #5, spade-linux).
#
# WHY. Every digest in this corpus moved, and the reason is that until now they
# were not digests of THE ENGINE -- they were digests of the engine PLUS
# whichever C runtime's libm happened to generate them.
#
# rng::Stream::next_gauss() built its Box-Muller pair with std::log, std::cos
# and std::sin. IEEE 754 mandates correct rounding for +, -, *, / and sqrt and
# mandates NOTHING for those three, so the MSVC CRT and glibc -- both excellent,
# both well under one ulp -- disagree by one ulp on roughly one argument in a
# hundred. Every OTHER operation in the engine is an IEEE-mandated one, which is
# exactly what makes the pinned op orders meaningful; these three were the sole
# exception, and this corpus silently recorded the MSVC answer to them.
#
# HOW IT SURFACED, AND WHY ONLY ONE SCENARIO SURFACED IT. The first Linux/gcc-13
# CI run on this branch failed `bounce` ALONE. Instrumented per-array, per-tick
# digests localized it exactly: of the sixteen registered arrays, ONLY "dryden"
# differed, and it differed at TICK 0 -- inside dryden_init(), before a single
# substep of physics had run. "bodies" was bit-identical on both platforms for
# all 901 ticks, so no trajectory ever diverged at all.
#
# The entire divergence was two ulps, both in dryden_init's five draws:
#
#     cos(0x3FA9EA7E)   MSVC 0x3E76B74D   glibc 0x3E76B74E   -> bounce w1 Dryden w0
#     sin(0x3F8A17F6)   MSVC 0x3F61A49F   glibc 0x3F61A49E   -> bounce w3 Dryden u
#
# bounce carries FOUR Dryden streams (its restitution ladder's four worlds) to
# the other scenarios' one, one and two, so it simply had the most init draws
# exposed: eight world-inits exist across the corpus, and two of bounce's four
# landed on a disputed argument. The other three scenarios passed on gcc by
# LUCK, not by contract. Their per-SUBSTEP draws differ between the platforms
# too, but the Dryden filter's noise gain (l ~ 7e-3 on the longitudinal channel,
# ~1.4e-7 on the transverse position-like state) attenuates a one-ulp
# perturbation of a draw to far below one ulp of the state it lands in, so those
# absorb. An INIT draw lands in the state raw, unattenuated -- which is why the
# divergence is at tick 0 and nowhere after it.
#
# THE FIX. core/fp32_math.hpp -- log32/sin32/cos32, built exclusively from
# IEEE-mandated operations over pinned groupings and compiled exactly once, into
# spade_core under -ffp-contract=off. next_gauss() now calls those. std::sqrt is
# deliberately KEPT: IEEE mandates correct rounding for it, so it is already
# bit-identical everywhere and replacing it would trade a guarantee for a
# polynomial.
#
# WHY THIS MOVED ALL FOUR DIGESTS AND NOT ONLY bounce's. The new primitives are
# ~1 ulp accurate -- tests/test_fp32_math.cpp proves that EXHAUSTIVELY, over all
# 2^24 arguments next_float() can produce and all 2^24 Box-Muller angles, not by
# sampling -- which is the same quality as the libms they replace but not the
# same BITS. Every gaussian in every scenario therefore changes in its last bit
# or two, and every digest moves with it. That is the expected consequence of
# removing a platform dependency, NOT a physics change: no pass, no op order, no
# state layout, no schedule position, and no part of the rng CONSTRUCTION (draw
# counts, draw order, the clamp, the sin-first convention) was touched. The
# corpus's physical-sanity test (CorpusScenariosProduceFiniteAndPhysicallySane-
# States) and all 359 other tests pass unchanged on both platforms.
#
# WHAT IS DIFFERENT ABOUT THIS REGENERATION. Every previous one recorded a value
# THIS BOX produces. This one records a value BOTH PLATFORMS produce: the digest
# below is verified green on spade-windows (MSVC 19.44) and spade-linux
# (gcc-13) in the same CI run. That is the property this corpus was always meant
# to have and, until this commit, never actually had.
#
#   previous digest  0x062f48aa065d71f4   (Task 18, generated 2026-08-10) -- an MSVC-libm value#
# PROVENANCE
#   generated  2026-08-11 (platform-libm parity fix; first generated at Task 16)
#   branch     spade/s1-s4, on top of 8c2543c
#   toolchain  MSVC 19.44 (VS 2022 Community) x64, /fp:precise, Ninja
#   cross-check gcc-13, Release, -ffp-contract=off (spade-linux CI job) produces
#              this same value -- the first digest in this corpus's history
#              pinned by two independent toolchains rather than one
#   presets    msvc-ninja-release AND msvc-ninja-debug produce this same value
#              (checked before committing -- the digest is a property of the
#              engine, not of the optimizer)
0xb786bbc01616cd4d
